7	\paragraph{Prompt-based mitigation.}
8	Building on the five-model type-specific mitigation study ($-$8.5\,pp pooled; Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}), we test whether disambiguation can be prompted at finer granularity. We evaluate generic awareness prompting (``the policy may contain ambiguous clauses; ask for clarification'') and type-specific prompting (targeting incompleteness, authorization scope, and lexical ambiguity individually) on GPT-5.4 with matched-pair McNemar tests ($n = 50$ per type). Generic prompting produces no overall reduction ($\Delta = +1.3$\,pp, $p > 0.5$). Type-specific results are asymmetric: authorization scope shows a marginal reduction ($-12$\,pp, $p = 0.070$), lexical ambiguity is unchanged ($-2$\,pp), and incompleteness \emph{worsens} ($+14$\,pp, $p = 0.118$)---suggesting that prompting agents to attend to missing information may trigger confabulation rather than caution ($p = 0.118$). These findings indicate that prompt-level intervention cannot remediate structural policy ambiguity, underscoring the need for policy-design-level solutions.
